 "title": "Handling Price Objections in Cold Calling",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator demonstrates how to handle a prospect asking for a fixed price during a cold call by flipping the question back to them. The main lesson is to avoid locking into a fixed price upfront and instead anchor the discussion on the prospect's specific requirements.",
 "key_points": [
  "The creator (Isshanth from InBooked) calls an interior and construction firm to offer pre-qualified site visits on a pay-on-result basis.",
  "When the prospect asks for a basic starting price, the creator flips the question by asking how much it would cost if he wanted interior or construction work done with them.",
  "The prospect responds that pricing depends on client requirements and space (e.g., 2 BHK vs 3 BHK), stating a 2 BHK might cost around 5 lakhs for a basic wardrobe, locked modular kitchen, and TV unit.",
  "The creator uses the prospect's own logic back against them, stating that his service pricing also completely depends on the client's requirements and he cannot throw around generic prices.",
  "The creator explains that his pricing is a fixed rate per booked meeting/call, not a percentage.",
  "When the prospect asks for a guarantee of clients, the creator explicitly states they cannot assure clients because it depends on whether the prospect can close them."
 ],
 "content": "She asked a 17-year-old for a price. I flipped it. But it depends on my requirement, right? Yeah, yeah. Depends on your requirement. So, same thing with my service as well, ma'am. The price completely depends on your [unclear]. Hello. Hi, [unclear]. Yes. Yeah, how are you? Yeah, good. Who is who is this? Yeah, this is Isshanth calling from InBooked. Is this a good time to talk? Okay, regarding about? Yeah, we're actually a company that helps interior and construction firms get pre-qualified site visits straight onto their calendar without any work on their side and is on a pay on result basis so no cost on your side. Okay. Yeah, just thinking. Okay. What is the basic starting range? Starting range, right? Uh let me just ask you, uh if I wanted to do some interior work with you, or construction work with you, how how much would it cost? It's according to the client requirement, it's according to the space what they give us. No, no, if I just want a number, just a number. Two, since 2 BHK is uh slightly different, 3 BHK is uh slightly differences, according to the requirements, it would change. Okay, can you just give a fixed price, like how much it would cost for me if I wanted tomorrow? Tomorrow? Maybe uh some 2 BHK it cost around 5 lakhs. But it depends on my requirement, right? Yeah, yeah. Depends on your requirement. I'm just saying the basic wardrobe, locked modular kitchen, TV unit, that's it. Yeah, yeah. So, same same thing with my service as well, ma'am. The price completely depends on your requirement. Uh I can't just throw around prices and then if we if we go on with this and then I tell you the actual price, then you might think, no, no, you told a different price at the start. Okay, but you have a percentage, right? Oh, yeah, it's not a percentage, it's actually a fixed rate on only the booked calls, uh booked meetings that you pay. Uh can we book something for tomorrow in the evening maybe? Yeah, you'll come to our office no ma'am no ma'am 